Subject: [PATCH] Preserve C++11 constexpr construction for trivial storage

TL_OPTIONAL_11_CONSTEXPR is empty in C++11 because constexpr member functions
implicitly become const. Constructors have no such restriction. The storage
specialization for trivially destructible values can keep its default and
in-place constructors constexpr, as the public optional constructors expect.

Empty construction initializes the dummy union member, not T; it does not
require T to be default-constructible. Non-trivial storage is unchanged.
---
 include/tl/optional.hpp | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/tl/optional.hpp b/include/tl/optional.hpp
--- a/include/tl/optional.hpp
+++ b/include/tl/optional.hpp
@@ -383,3 +383,3 @@
 template <class T> struct optional_storage_base<T, true> {
-  TL_OPTIONAL_11_CONSTEXPR optional_storage_base() noexcept
+  constexpr optional_storage_base() noexcept
       : m_dummy(), m_has_value(false) {}
@@ -387,3 +387,3 @@
   template <class... U>
-  TL_OPTIONAL_11_CONSTEXPR optional_storage_base(in_place_t, U &&... u)
+  constexpr optional_storage_base(in_place_t, U &&... u)
       : m_value(std::forward<U>(u)...), m_has_value(true) {}
